{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_72","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -f cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Implementation notes: iterative divide off ALU critical path\n\nWhat changed:\n- Removed real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU `/` and `%` operators from the combinational ALU. The ALU still keeps RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas for formal.\n- Added `iter_divider.sv`, a restoring 32-bit iterative divider with RV32M divide-by-zero and signed-overflow behavior.\n- Wired `ex_stage.sv` to launch the divider for real DIV/REM ops, bubble EX/MEM while the divider runs, and capture the final result into the normal EX/MEM register when ready.\n- Added an EX-stage stall input to `hazard_unit.sv` and connected it in `core.sv` so IF/ID and ID/EX hold during iterative divide/remainder operations.\n- Added `00_core_pkg.sv` as a guarded declaration-order shim so the requested `rtl/*.sv` Verilator glob lint sees typedefs before modules.\n- Split cocotb arithmetic coverage: `test_alu.py` covers one-cycle ALU ops and multiply; `test_iter_divider.py` covers DIV/REM vectors.\n\nDeviations:\n- In RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS builds, DIV/REM are intentionally not intercepted by the iterative divider; the ALU ALTOPS formulas remain visible to match riscv-formal's substituted M-extension specs.\n- The divider lets older MEM/WB work drain by inserting EX/MEM bubbles during the divide instead of holding a stale EX/MEM instruction for the whole divide.\n\nConcerns:\n- Ordinary non-special divides take 34-ish stall cycles from EX launch to EX/MEM capture. This should be fine for CoreMark-like workloads but is intentionally slower for division-heavy code.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` in this checkout defaulted the harness to `NRET=2` and failed at C++ compile because this core is declared `nret: 1`. Rerunning with `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` matches `core.yaml` and passed.\n\nLocal status:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass.\n- `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_alu.py cores/bench/test/test_iter_divider.py`: pass, 2 passed.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: pass, 2 ELFs tested.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
