{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Registered divider stall island\n================================\n\nChanged `ex_stage.sv` so the divider's live `busy`/`valid` state is no longer\nexported as the front-end stall. DIV/REM now set a registered\n`div_front_stall_q` when the instruction is admitted into EX1, and that\nregistered bit drives the hazard unit until the divider result is captured into\nEX/MEM.\n\nThe divider hold/capture logic remains local to EX via `div_ex_hold` and\n`div_capture`. On the result-capture edge EX1 is cleared instead of consuming\nthe held ID/EX payload, which avoids duplicating the next instruction while IF\nand ID are still held by the registered front-stall bit. `core.sv` now uses an\nEX-provided `ex1_advances` signal for the registered forwarding tags, so a\ndependent instruction still forwards a just-captured divider result correctly\nafter the cold bubble.\n\n`hazard_unit.sv` behavior is otherwise unchanged: RAW interlocks, redirects,\nimem stalls, and dmem stalls keep their previous priority and gating. The\ninterface comment now describes the EX input as a registered long-op front-end\nhold.\n\nAdded `test/test_divider_stall.py` with focused cocotb coverage for:\n- DIV with instruction-memory backpressure and a dependent consumer.\n- REM while a prior load holds dmem long enough for the divider to finish\n  before EX/MEM capture is allowed.\n\nDeviations and concerns:\n- I kept one combinational EX-local \"result advances\" output to the forwarding\n  tag register. This preserves dependent DIV/REM forwarding without adding an\n  extra post-capture stall. The hazard/front-end stall path is the registered\n  path targeted by the hypothesis.\n- DIV/REM now intentionally pay one cold bubble because EX1 is cleared on the\n  capture edge while ID/EX remains held.\n\nLocal checks:\n- Verilator lint: pass.\n  `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`\n- New cocotb divider stall test: pass.\n  `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_divider_stall.py`\n- Formal: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n  `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`\n- Cosim: the plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` command failed before simulation\n  because the harness compiled with `-DNRET=2` against this `nret: 1` core and\n  looked for channel-1 RVFI ports. Rerunning with the matching setting passed:\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` reported `\"passed\": true` and\n  `\"elfs_tested\": 2`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
