{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"## Decoupled sequential fetch queue\n\nChanged `rtl/if_stage.sv` from a combinational IF/ID handoff into a two-entry\nregistered FIFO with an independent `fetch_pc`. The fetch side pushes\n`pc/instr` when `io_imemReady` is high and the queue has space, including the\ncycle a full queue is popped. Redirects flush both entries and restart fetch at\nthe resolved target.\n\nUpdated `rtl/hazard_unit.sv` so `io_imemReady` no longer freezes the whole\npipeline. Empty fetch slots reach decode as `IF/ID.valid=0` bubbles; load-use,\ndmem stalls, and divider stalls hold the queue head by preventing the consume\npulse. Redirect and load-use still bubble ID/EX precisely.\n\nUpdated `rtl/core.sv` to wire `io_imemReady` into IF and to generate the queue\nconsume pulse when the IF/ID head is valid and decode is neither stalled nor\nflushed. No package or RVFI bundle changes were needed.\n\nDeviations: the queue is strictly sequential and does not provide an empty-FIFO\ncombinational bypass from `io_imemData` to decode. This preserves the\nregistered boundary but adds one frontend fill cycle after reset or redirect.\n\nConcerns: repeated redirect assertion while an older dmem stall holds the branch\nin EX will keep the queue flushed until the backend can advance, matching the\nexisting redirect priority but limiting fetch-ahead in that specific overlap.\n\nLocal checks:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench`: harness built with `NRET=2` and failed because this core exposes only nret=1 ports\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: pass, 2 ELF tests\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented the two-entry sequential fetch queue in `if_stage.sv`, decoupled imem readiness in `hazard_unit.sv`, and wired queue consume in `core.sv`. Notes are in `cores/bench/implementation_notes.md`.\n\nChecks: Verilator lint passed, formal passed `53/0`, and `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed. Plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` failed before simulation because the local harness compiled with `NRET=2` for this nret=1 core."}}
