{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"## Tiny Loop Replay Predictor\n\nImplemented a single-entry, eight-instruction loop replay predictor:\n\n- Added `loop_replay_predictor.sv` with an eight-entry accepted-fetch history.\n- Allocates only taken direct backward conditional branches when the branch is\n  the newest history entry and the target-to-branch span is contiguous.\n- Stores replay instructions and PCs in ascending loop-body order.\n- Extended IF/ID and ID/EX metadata with replay-active, replay-loop-end, and\n  replay restart PC fields.\n- IF enters replay on a target-PC hit, feeds buffered instructions/PCs, ignores\n  `io_imemReady` through the hazard unit while replaying, and keeps the\n  external fetch address parked on the loop fall-through.\n- EX suppresses the redirect only when a replay-end branch really retakes the\n  recorded restart target. Loop exits, target mismatches, jumps, or malformed\n  replay-end instructions redirect to the resolved next PC and cancel replay.\n\nDeviations:\n\n- Kept the predictor single-entry rather than adding replacement state; this\n  keeps IF/EX wiring and next-PC pressure small.\n- Did not add a separate target mux in IF. Replay only restarts the local\n  buffered stream, and normal EX redirects remain the precise recovery path.\n- Left replay active across load-use, dmem, and divider holds by freezing the\n  replay index with the existing stall signals.\n\nConcerns:\n\n- The extra replay instruction/PC mux feeds IF/ID decode input, so timing may\n  still depend on whether Gowin maps the 8-way selects cheaply.\n- The single replay entry can be overwritten by any later short backward loop;\n  this is intentional, but it means only the hottest last-seen loop benefits.\n\nLocal verification:\n\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv` passed.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` passed:\n  53 passed, 0 failed.\n- Plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` failed before simulation because the make\n  dependency builds `test/cosim/main.cpp` with default `NRET=2` while this core\n  declares `nret: 1`. Reran with the matching contract:\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed, testing 2 ELFs.\n- `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py` passed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
