{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'if [ -f cores/bench/LESSONS.md ]; then nl -ba cores/bench/LESSONS.md; fi'","aggregated_output":"     1\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r1s0 (improvement, +825.33%): Moving rare DIV/REM operations from the one-cycle ALU output mux into an EX-stage iterative unit can dramatically improve FPGA Fmax because CoreMark keeps the common ADD/address path hot while the deep divider cone mostly only taxes timing.\n     2\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r1s1 (regression, +731.68%): Moving hot MUL/MULH operations into the same EX-holding multi-cycle path as DIV/REM can swamp any ALU timing relief on CoreMark; keep multiplier latency hidden or separately pipelined when only the divider cone is the cold timing problem.\n     3\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 (regression, +3.63%): A tiny IF-side BHT/direct-JAL next-PC predictor can regress CoreMark when its `io_imemData` decode and target mux sit on the fetch PC-update path, because the added timing/metadata cost can outweigh recovered taken-back-edge flushes.\n     4\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r2s0 (regression, -10.30%): A dedicated LOAD/STORE address adder can regress CoreMark when it duplicates the same rs1+imm carry chain and adds EX result-select routing instead of removing the true regfile-to-dmem address bottleneck.\n     5\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Retiming branch resolution into EX2 requires redirect kills to flush the new EX1/EX2 register as well as IF/ID and ID/EX, otherwise wrong-path instructions already in EX1 can survive stall-heavy cosim.\n     6\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r2s2 (regression, -15.58%): Even without IF instruction decode, a tagged BTB still puts tag/counter/target-table lookup plus a target mux on the fetch PC-update path, and CoreMark's recovered taken-branch bubbles did not repay that timing cost.\n     7\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r3s0 (regression, +2.55%): Opcode-qualified load-use gating did not repay its added fetched-opcode decode and raw-imem comparator control, implying CoreMark's false immediate-bit load-use bubbles are rarer than the timing cost of making hazard detection more precise.\n     8\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.56%): A fall-through-only two-entry fetch queue adds `valid/full/consume` frontend control around `if_id_w` while EX-stage redirects still flush queued sequential instructions, so CoreMark's random `io_imemReady` overlap did not repay the extra bookkeeping.\n     9\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r3s2 (regression, -7.88%): Even zero-state backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction regressed because IF-side immediate decode, target adders, range checks, and next-PC muxing on the fetch path cost more than CoreMark's recovered loop-back redirect bubbles.\n    10\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r4s0 (regression, -7.43%): Splitting ADD/address into an EX fast path can still regress when `fast_result` control and an `ex_result` mux remain after the adder while the side ALU stays live, so avoiding post-adder selection on the rs1/imm-to-EX/MEM path matters more than just trimming the ALU case mux.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
