{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r4s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Single-entry in-order store buffer\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is mostly Fmax-limited after DIV/REM were removed from\n  the common ALU path, and the failed predictor/fetch-queue attempts show that\n  frontend speculation is too expensive for this FPGA target. One remaining\n  structural source of lost CoreMark cycles is that every store in MEM freezes\n  the whole in-order pipeline whenever the randomized dmem bus deasserts\n  ready, even though stores produce no register result.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry in-order store buffer at the load/store boundary. When\n  EX/MEM holds a data-RAM store and the buffer is empty, capture its address,\n  replicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI fields into the buffer, retire the\n  store through MEM/WB, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while\n  the buffer drives dmem until io_dmemReady accepts it. A following load,\n  following store, FENCE, EBREAK/SYSTEM instruction, or any MMIO/marker access\n  must wait until the buffer is empty, and MMIO stores themselves should bypass\n  the buffer and complete only on the external bus. This preserves in-order\n  memory visibility and CoreMark timing markers while hiding random dmem stalls\n  on ordinary RAM stores. Because the enqueue decision sits behind the existing\n  EX/MEM register and no target prediction is added to IF, the IPC gain should\n  have less Fmax risk than previous structural attempts.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add the one-entry store buffer, data-RAM/MMIO classification, buffered dmem drive, strict drain-before-load/store/FENCE/EBREAK behavior, and RVFI retirement of buffered stores at enqueue.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace raw dmem-ready pipeline freezing with an LSU stall input that only blocks on loads, unbufferable stores, full-buffer conflicts, ordered drain points, and active memory hazards.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the MEM-stage LSU stall/buffer status into hazard control while preserving the existing nret=1 top-level IO and RVFI port set.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add any small control or payload fields needed to mark ordered instructions and carry store-buffer RVFI metadata cleanly.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror any core_pkg.sv bundle additions for alphabetical build flows.\"\n  - file: test/test_store_buffer.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for buffered RAM stores, load-after-store draining, MMIO marker bypass, and EBREAK/FENCE drain ordering under dmem stalls.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
