{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r2s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-001-r2s0\ntitle: \"Dedicated load-store address adder\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  After DIV/REM moved out of the combinational ALU, the remaining best-known\n  timing path is much shorter but still runs from the ID/regfile side through\n  EX and the ALU into the data-memory address path. CoreMark has frequent\n  loads and stores, so the effective-address calculation is both hot and\n  timing-sensitive. Today those addresses use the full RV32IM ALU result mux,\n  even though a memory address only needs rs1 plus the sign-extended immediate.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a dedicated effective-address adder in ex_stage.sv for LOAD and STORE\n  operations and use it directly for EX/MEM.alu_result when ctrl.mem_read or\n  ctrl.mem_write is set. Keep the existing ALU for integer arithmetic, shifts,\n  comparisons, branches, jumps, and one-cycle MUL variants, preserving the\n  CoreMark IPC profile and the successful iterative DIV/REM design. This should\n  shorten the hot dmem address path by removing the ALU op decode, multiplier\n  result alternatives, shift/compare alternatives, and broad result mux from\n  the memory-address cone while changing only local EX-stage combinational\n  selection.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute a dedicated LOAD/STORE effective address as forwarded rs1 plus the decoded immediate and select it into EX/MEM.alu_result for memory operations instead of routing memory addresses through the general ALU output mux.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
