{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r3s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-001-r3s0\ntitle: \"Tighter load-use hazard gating\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current best core already removed the dominant DIV/REM cone, and recent\n  experiments show that larger predictor and execute-retiming ideas can add\n  more timing risk than CoreMark reward. The remaining control logic still\n  treats the next instruction's raw rs1 and rs2 bit fields as real operands\n  for every opcode, and it derives those fields through the IF-stage\n  flush/redirect NOP mux. That can create unnecessary one-cycle load-use\n  bubbles on I-type, load, upper-immediate, jump, fence, or system\n  instructions whose immediate bits happen to match a preceding load's rd,\n  and it leaves a timing arc from EX redirect through IF into the hazard\n  unit and back to the ID/EX register controls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Rework only the load-use hazard qualifier so it checks actual source\n  operands for the fetched instruction opcode: rs1 for OP, OP-IMM, LOAD,\n  STORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 only for OP, STORE, and BRANCH. Gate the\n  comparison off when imem is not ready or when EX is redirecting, and feed\n  the hazard unit from the raw fetched instruction bits plus those explicit\n  valid/redirect qualifiers rather than from the IF-stage instruction after\n  the redirect/flush NOP mux. This should remove false CoreMark bubbles after\n  loads while shortening the redirect-to-ID control loop seen in recent\n  timing reports. The pipeline topology, RVFI behavior, memory handshake, and\n  successful iterative DIV/REM design remain unchanged.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the unconditional rs1/rs2 load-use comparison with opcode-aware source-use qualification, gated by imem readiness and redirect so wrong-path or unavailable fetch data cannot request a load-use stall.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Pass the raw fetched instruction or opcode/source fields and the needed validity qualifiers into the hazard unit, keeping the external core IO and nret=1 RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
