{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-001-r3s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-001-r4s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-001-r5s0\\\") | .id+\\\"\\\\n\\\"+(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)+\\\"\\\\nCHANGES:\\\\n\\\"+(.changes|map(\\\"- \\\"+.file+\\\": \\\"+.description)|join(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-001-r3s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-001-r4s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-001-r5s0\\\") | .id+\\\"\\\\n\\\"+(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)+\\\"\\\\nCHANGES:\\\\n\\\"+(.changes|map(\\\"- \\\"+.file+\\\": \\\"+.description)|join(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-001-r3s0\nRework only the load-use hazard qualifier so it checks actual source\noperands for the fetched instruction opcode: rs1 for OP, OP-IMM, LOAD,\nSTORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 only for OP, STORE, and BRANCH. Gate the\ncomparison off when imem is not ready or when EX is redirecting, and feed\nthe hazard unit from the raw fetched instruction bits plus those explicit\nvalid/redirect qualifiers rather than from the IF-stage instruction after\nthe redirect/flush NOP mux. This should remove false CoreMark bubbles after\nloads while shortening the redirect-to-ID control loop seen in recent\ntiming reports. The pipeline topology, RVFI behavior, memory handshake, and\nsuccessful iterative DIV/REM design remain unchanged.\n\nCHANGES:\n- rtl/hazard_unit.sv: Replace the unconditional rs1/rs2 load-use comparison with opcode-aware source-use qualification, gated by imem readiness and redirect so wrong-path or unavailable fetch data cannot request a load-use stall.\n- rtl/core.sv: Pass the raw fetched instruction or opcode/source fields and the needed validity qualifiers into the hazard unit, keeping the external core IO and nret=1 RVFI contract unchanged.\nhyp-20261004-001-r4s0\nSplit the EX-stage result generation into a narrow fast add/sub/address path\nfor ADD, ADDI, AUIPC, LOAD, STORE, and JALR target arithmetic, and a separate\nside ALU for logic, shifts, comparisons, LUI, and one-cycle MUL variants.\nThe fast path should be the only path selected for memory effective addresses\nand ordinary integer additions, so those operations no longer traverse the\nbroad RV32IM ALU case mux that also contains shift, compare, LUI, and multiply\nalternatives. Unlike the failed dedicated load-store adder experiment, this\nreplaces the hot add result path rather than duplicating it beside the old\nALU result and then adding another selector. DIV/REM stay in the existing\niterative unit. CoreMark should keep the same cycle count while Fmax improves\nfrom a shorter add/address-to-EX/MEM path.\n\nCHANGES:\n- rtl/ex_stage.sv: Compute and select a dedicated fast add/sub/address result for add-like ALU ops and memory effective addresses, using the existing forwarded operands and preserving branch, jump, and iterative DIV/REM behavior.\n- rtl/alu.sv: Remove the hot ADD/SUB/LUI-style cases from the broad one-cycle side ALU or demote them to non-critical defaults, leaving logic, shifts, comparisons, and one-cycle multiply variants off the add/address cone.\n- rtl/core_pkg.sv: If needed, add a compact decoded result-select or add-sub flag to ctrl_t so EX can choose the fast path without re-decoding the full ALU opcode on the critical data path.\n- rtl/00_core_pkg.sv: Mirror any ctrl_t encoding change for alphabetic build flows that compile this guarded shim before core_pkg.sv.\nhyp-20261004-001-r5s0\nReplace the three parallel multiplier expressions in rtl/alu.sv with one shared 33x33 signed product whose operand sign bits are selected from the decoded MUL variant. MUL can use the low 32 bits, while MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU use bits 63:32 of the same mathematical product after choosing signed/signed, unsigned/unsigned, or signed/unsigned operand extension. Keep DIV/REM in the existing iterative divider and leave the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutes unchanged.\n\nThis should preserve CoreMark's cycle count and the successful one-cycle MUL behavior while reducing DSP/LUT use, multiplier output fan-in, and routing load on the ALU result mux. Because it removes redundant arithmetic rather than adding a new post-adder selector, it targets the current timing lesson more directly than the failed fast-address and dedicated-address experiments.\n\nCHANGES:\n- rtl/alu.sv: Collapse mul_ss, mul_uu, and mul_su into a single signed 33x33 product selected by the MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU op, preserving one-cycle MUL latency and leaving ALTOPS behavior unchanged.\n- test/test_alu.py: Add focused RV32M multiply edge vectors for signed high, unsigned high, signed-unsigned high, and low-half equivalence through negative operands.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
