aw-imem comparator control, implying CoreMark's false immediate-bit load-use bubbles are rarer than the timing cost of making hazard detection more precise.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.56%): A fall-throu\n…[5563 of 8635 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:822a5c29fe7216d8]…\nsquash cleanup must keep bubble gating narrower than the redirect-to-regfile path it removes.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r9s2 (regression, +1.21%): Registering only the conditional-branch frontend repair is too narrow a retime when `branch_redirect_d` still fans into local EX1 kill/seen control and CoreMark taken loops pay an extra recovery cycle; branch-path cleanup needs to remove live compare control more completely.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r10s0 (improvement, +8.70%): Adding a `DIV_POST` register for raw quotient/remainder separates the final restoring-divider subtract/compare from signed result selection, trading one cold DIV/REM cycle for higher CoreMark Fmax once `u_divider` is the worst path.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r10s2 (regression, -22.81%): One-cycle lookahead BTB reads still regressed because carrying `predicted_next_pc` metadata through IF/ID/EX and redirecting on an EX-wide resolved-next-PC compare adds enough control/register fanout that CoreMark loop-back wins do not repay it.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
