{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r11s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Registered bypass select retime\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst path is no longer the divider or the ALU result mux; it runs from `cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd` through `cpu/u_fwd` into `cpu/u_ex/ex1_q.branch_misalign`. That means the live forwarding comparator and source-select logic are now feeding the same-cycle EX1 operand mux, branch compare, target/misalign logic, and EX1 register input.\n\n  Prior successful changes in this core were structural retimes that kept CoreMark's hot instruction flow intact while cutting a timed cone. Prior predictor and LSU-buffer attempts repeatedly regressed by adding frontend or memory-port control, so the most promising structural slot is a local bypass-network retime with no new speculation, queues, or memory-port muxing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the purely combinational forwarding selector with a registered next-EX1 bypass decision. When an instruction is accepted into or held in ID/EX, compute source tags for rs1 and rs2 that describe where each operand must come from on the cycle it will be captured by EX1: ID/EX value, EX/MEM ALU result, or MEM/WB writeback result. Update those tags in lockstep with the existing RAW, dmem, divider, flush, and redirect controls, including lookahead cases where an EX1 producer will become EX/MEM or an EX/MEM load will become MEM/WB before the consumer advances.\n\n  EX1 would then use registered 2-bit source tags to drive the existing operand muxes. The data muxes remain, but the rd/wen comparators and priority logic no longer sit in the same timed arc as branch condition, JALR target, branch misalign, and ALU operand capture. This should shorten the current `u_mem -> u_fwd -> u_ex` critical path without adding pipeline stages or extra CoreMark dependency stalls. RVFI timing remains verification-only, and the nret=1 top-level contract is unchanged.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Restructure the forwarding unit into a registered/lookahead bypass-selector generator with stable rs1/rs2 source tags for the next EX1 capture, rather than live rd-comparator outputs feeding EX1 directly.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Expose or reuse the existing advance, hold, bubble, flush, dmem-stall, and divider-stall decisions so bypass tags update exactly when the matching ID/EX payload updates.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Consume registered bypass source tags for the EX1 operand muxes while preserving the current EX1/EX2 branch metadata retime, divider handshake, redirect behavior, and stall holds.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the retimed bypass-selector state between the hazard/forwarding logic and execute stage without changing the core IO or single-channel RVFI interface.\"\n  - file: test/test_registered_forwarding.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for ALU-to-branch, load-to-branch, store-data, JALR, dmem-stall, imem-stall, and redirect interactions that rely on the retimed forwarding tags.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
