{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r12s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Decouple ID stalls from regfile reads\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst path now starts in EX/divider control and\n  reaches back through ID into the register file. One likely contributor is\n  the ID-stage stall behavior: when ID/EX is held, the stall signal selects\n  held source register addresses onto the regfile read ports so the operand\n  values can be refreshed. That makes cold execute stall logic part of a hot\n  regfile address/read cone.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change the ID/EX hold structure so stalls no longer repoint the register\n  file read ports at the held instruction. During a stall, keep the ID/EX\n  payload in place and update each held operand only when the WB-stage write\n  address matches that operand's source register, using the writeback data\n  directly. The regfile read ports can then remain driven by the current\n  fetched instruction fields rather than by a stall-controlled mux.\n\n  This preserves the freshness fix that motivated operand refresh: if an\n  unrelated dependency causes ID/EX to wait while another source writes back,\n  the matching held operand still captures the new value. Forwarding still\n  covers EX/MEM and MEM/WB results when the consumer finally advances. The\n  expected timing benefit is removing the EX/divider-stall-to-ID-regfile\n  read-address path without adding a pipeline stage, predictor metadata, or\n  memory-port muxing.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add WB write address/data inputs and replace stall-time regfile rereads with per-source writeback-match operand updates while the ID/EX register is held.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the WB-stage writeback enable, destination register, and data into id_stage so held operands can be refreshed without using stall-controlled regfile read addresses.\"\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Keep the normal combinational read/write-first behavior for newly decoded instructions; verify no new stall-controlled read-address muxing is needed on the regfile interface.\"\n  - file: test/test_registered_forwarding.py\n    description: \"Cover a held ID/EX instruction with one source updated by WB while another dependency or dmem/divider stall keeps the instruction from advancing.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
