{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r14s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r14s1\ntitle: \"Late store-data capture lane\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current best core has already retimed forwarding, branch metadata, and\n  divider control; recent regressions show that extra predictor or divider\n  bookkeeping is unlikely to clear the acceptance margin. A remaining structural\n  cost is that STORE treats rs2 data like an EX-stage operand even though only\n  rs1 is needed to compute the address in EX; the actual store data is not\n  consumed until the instruction reaches MEM and drives dmem_wdata.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split STORE operand handling into an early address lane and a late data lane.\n  STORE should continue to read and forward rs1 in EX1/EX2 for the effective\n  address, but rs2 should no longer force the store to wait merely so its data\n  can be captured before EX. Instead, carry the store's rs2 register number and\n  a stale fallback value with the pipeline payload, then select the final store\n  data in EX2 just before writing EX/MEM.write_data, using a small late bypass\n  from the current EX/MEM ALU result or MEM/WB writeback result when those\n  producers match rs2.\n\n  The hazard unit should still stall a store when rs1 is unavailable, and it\n  should still stall for an immediately preceding load whose data cannot reach\n  the late lane in time. But it can stop treating EX1 ALU producers and EX/MEM\n  loads that match only store rs2 as address hazards, because their values are\n  available by the time the store data is finalized. This is a structural\n  pipeline split rather than broad source-use prediction: no fetch predictor,\n  no store buffer, and no mux on the wrapper-visible dmem address or write\n  enable shape.\n\n  CoreMark's optimized code performs many stores of freshly computed list,\n  matrix, and state values. Avoiding unnecessary store-data bubbles should\n  improve iterations per cycle, while keeping the late bypass local to store\n  write-data capture limits the Fmax risk compared with prior full source-use\n  gating and LSU-buffer experiments.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Distinguish store address hazards from store-data hazards: keep rs1 interlocks, keep the immediately-preceding load-to-store-data stall, but allow stores to advance when only rs2 can be satisfied by the late data lane.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add late store-data selection before EX/MEM capture, overriding write_data and rs2_val for stores from EX/MEM or MEM/WB producers when rs2 matches, while leaving address generation and branch/divider behavior unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"If needed, add compact metadata for late store-data validity/source tracking while preserving the existing nret=1 retirement payload shape.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror any pipeline-bundle field additions from core_pkg.sv for alphabetical build flows.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Keep normal EX1 operand forwarding intact, but ensure registered rs2 tags do not force extra work for stores whose data will be resolved by the late lane.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire any added store-data hazard qualifiers or bypass inputs locally between hazard, forwarding, and execute without changing the top-level memory or RVFI IO.\"\n  - file: test/test_late_store_data.py\n    description: \"Add directed cases for ALU-to-store-data, load-to-store-data, store-address dependencies, dmem stalls, and RVFI rs2/mem_wdata correctness.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
