raw-imem comparator control, implying CoreMark's false immediate-bit load-use bubbles are rarer than the timing cost of making hazard detection more precise.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.56%): A fall-throu\n…[8613 of 11685 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ad2109eaa86f87e4]…\nmuxing and the extra registered wakeup cycle outweigh removing `iter_divider.valid` from the global stall cone.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r14s0 (regression, -0.86%): Predecoding DIV/REM into `ctrl_t.is_divrem` did not beat the existing `alu_op` compare because the added control bit widens the decode/ID-EX payload and still feeds the same divider-stall fanout; prune live control cones only when the removed decode is truly dominant.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r14s1 (regression, -6.40%): Late STORE rs2 capture did not repay its EX2 write-data mux, EX/MEM/WB bypass tags, and misalign-aware hazard bookkeeping, so CoreMark's store-data bubbles are cheaper than widening the already-hot store/address control path.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r14s2 (regression, -10.85%): Extending DIV/REM predecode to `div_signed`/`div_wants_remainder` widened `ctrl_t` and divider-control fanout more than it saved inside `iter_divider`, so cold op decode removal is not worth extra ID/EX payload bits on the global stall path.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
