{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 8 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple ID stalls from regfile reads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current champion's worst path now starts in EX/divider control and\\nreaches back through ID into the register file. One likely contributor is\\nthe ID-stage stall behavior: when ID/EX is held, the stall signal selects\\nheld source register addresses onto the regfile read ports so the operand\\nvalues can be refreshed. That makes cold execute stall logic part of a hot\\nregfile address/read cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current champion's worst path now starts in EX/divider control and\\nreaches back through ID into the register file. One likely contributor is\\nthe ID-stage stall behavior: when ID/EX is held, the stall signal selects\\nheld source register addresses onto the regfile read ports so the operand\\nvalues can be refreshed. That makes cold execute stall logic part of a hot\\nregfile address/read cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Change the ID/EX hold structure so stalls no longer repoint the register\\nfile read ports at the held instruction. During a stall, keep the ID/EX\\npayload in place and update each held operand only when the WB-stage write\\naddress matches that operand's source register, using the writeback data\\ndirectly. The regfile read ports can then remain driven by the current\\nfetched instruction fields rather than by a stall-controlled mux.\\n\\nThis preserves the freshness fix that motivated operand refresh: if an\\nunrelated dependency causes ID/EX to wait while another source writes back,\\nthe matching held operand still captures the new value. Forwarding still\\ncovers EX/MEM and MEM/WB results when the consumer finally advances. The\\nexpected timing benefit is removing the EX/divider-stall-to-ID-