{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r3s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r3s1\ntitle: \"Decoupled sequential fetch queue\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current core exposes a combinational IF/ID payload and injects a frontend\n  bubble every time the random instruction bus deasserts io_imemReady. During\n  dmem stalls, load-use bubbles, divider stalls, and redirect recovery, the\n  backend often cannot consume an instruction, but the existing PC path simply\n  holds instead of using ready imem cycles to fetch ahead. Prior predictor\n  attempts recovered some CoreMark cycles but lost too much Fmax by adding\n  target lookup and next-PC muxing on the fetch path; a purely sequential\n  fetch buffer attacks the stall overlap opportunity without predicting\n  control flow.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with a two-entry sequential fetch\n  queue. The fetch side keeps an independent fetch_pc and pushes pc/instr\n  entries whenever io_imemReady is high and the queue is not full, while the\n  decode side pops one entry only when ID is allowed to advance. Redirects\n  from EX flush the queue and restart fetch_pc at the resolved target; there\n  is no BHT, BTB, or instruction-immediate decode in IF. This should let dmem\n  stalls, load-use bubbles, divider holds, and taken-branch bubbles prefill\n  one or two following instructions, so later random i-stalls are absorbed by\n  the queue instead of becoming CoreMark bubbles. Registering the IF/ID\n  boundary should also reduce the u_if/u_hazard/u_id feedback path seen in\n  recent timing reports, while preserving the existing single-retire RVFI\n  contract and EX-stage branch semantics.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Turn fetch into a two-entry sequential instruction FIFO with independent fetch_pc, decode pop, redirect flush, and queue-head IF/ID output.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Separate frontend fetch availability from decode stalls so an empty queue bubbles ID, backend stalls hold the queue head, and imemReady no longer directly freezes the whole pipeline.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the fetch-queue consume/empty control between IF, ID, and hazard logic while preserving the current top-level memory and RVFI ports.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Consume the registered queue head only when decode is not stalled, keeping flush behavior precise on redirects and load-use bubbles.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"If needed, add minimal fetch-valid metadata to the existing IF/ID bundle without changing downstream RVFI-visible fields.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror any IF/ID bundle metadata change for alphabetical build flows.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
