mparator control, implying CoreMark's false immediate-bit load-use bubbles are rarer than the timing cost of making hazard detection more precise.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.56%): A fall-through-only two-entry fetch queue adds `valid/full/consume` frontend control around `if_id_w` while EX-stage redirects still flush queued sequential instructions, so CoreMark's random `io_imemReady` overlap did not repay the extra bookkeeping.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r3s2 (regression, -7.88%): Even zero-state backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction regressed because IF-side immediate decode, target adders, range checks, and next-PC muxing on the fetch path cost more than CoreMark's recovered loop-back redirect bubbles.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
