{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r4s1\\\") | \\\"--- \\\\(.id) \\\\(.title) ---\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\nERROR:\\\\n\\\\(.error // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"--- hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 Retimed two-stage execute path ---\nMOTIVATION:\nThe current champion removed the divider from the one-cycle ALU, but the\nreported worst path still runs through the register-file/forwarding path into\nthe execute ALU before reaching the next timed boundary. CoreMark is now more\nlikely limited by Fmax than by rare RV32M divide latency, so a structural\nretiming of the hot execute cone is the most promising remaining angle in\nthis slot.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nSplit the existing EX stage into EX1 and EX2. EX1 performs operand selection,\nMEM/WB and EX/MEM forwarding, branch compare input preparation, store-data\ncapture, and iterative-divider launch/hold bookkeeping, then registers the\nselected operands, immediate, decoded control, and PC. EX2 contains the\nactual RV32I ALU/address adder, one-cycle multiplier path, branch/jump target\ncalculation, misalignment checks, and the existing EX/MEM register update.\n\nThis adds one pipeline stage to the execute side and requires forwarding from\nthe new EX2/MEM boundary plus MEM/WB back to EX1, with load-use and\nlong-latency stalls updated accordingly. Taken branches and jumps gain one\ncycle of penalty, but the dominant FPGA path should shrink from\nregfile/forward muxes plus ALU/multiply output selection to one of those\nhalves. If Fmax rises by roughly 15-25%, the extra control-flow bubbles should\nstill clear the 4.6% acceptance threshold on CoreMark.\n\nNOTES:\n\nERROR:\ncosim_failed: random1.elf [--istall --dstall]\n--- hyp-20261004-002-r4s1 Single-entry in-order store buffer ---\nMOTIVATION:\nThe current champion is mostly Fmax-limited after DIV/REM were removed from\nthe common ALU path, and the failed predictor/fetch-queue attempts show that\nfrontend speculation is too expensive for this FPGA target. One remaining\nstructural source of lost CoreMark cycles is that every store in MEM freezes\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever the randomized dmem bus deasserts\nready, even though stores produce no register result.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nAdd a one-entry in-order store buffer at the load/store boundary. When\nEX/MEM holds a data-RAM store and the buffer is empty, capture its address,\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI fields into the buffer, retire the\nstore through MEM/WB, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while\nthe buffer drives dmem until io_dmemReady accepts it. A following load,\nfollowing store, FENCE, EBREAK/SYSTEM instruction, or any MMIO/marker access\nmust wait until the buffer is empty, and MMIO stores themselves should bypass\nthe buffer and complete only on the external bus. This preserves in-order\nmemory visibility and CoreMark timing markers while hiding random dmem stalls\non ordinary RAM stores. Because the enqueue decision sits behind the existing\nEX/MEM register and no target prediction is added to IF, the IPC gain should\nhave less Fmax risk than previous structural attempts.\n\nNOTES:\n\nERROR:\nplacement_failed\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
