{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r5s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"BRAM-safe store skid LSU\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current core still freezes the entire in-order pipeline whenever a store sits in MEM while the randomized dmem ready signal is low. Stores do not produce a register result, so ordinary RAM stores are the cleanest remaining opportunity to hide part of the 22% data-bus backpressure without touching the fragile fetch path. The previous store-buffer attempt failed placement because its replay mux disturbed Gowin's byte-lane RAM inference, so the structural idea should be retried only with a BRAM-safe LSU port shape.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure MEM into a tiny LSU request stage with a one-entry store skid slot that is the only source allowed to drive RAM store writes. Ordinary data-RAM stores enqueue their aligned address, replicated data, mask, and RVFI metadata into the slot, retire in order, and allow younger non-memory instructions to continue while the slot drains when io_dmemReady goes high. Loads, following stores, FENCE/EBREAK/SYSTEM, and MMIO/marker stores must wait until the slot is empty, and MMIO stores must complete directly on the external bus so CoreMark timing markers stay precise.\n\n  To avoid repeating the placement failure, do not priority-mux a replayed store with a live store on io_dmemWEn/io_dmemAddr. Instead drive the dmem write port from one registered store-request record, keep io_dmemWEn as either that record's byte mask or zero, and let the current EX/MEM path feed only the enqueue side. This preserves the wrapper's canonical single write-port RAM pattern while hiding store stalls behind independent ALU/control instructions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Convert MEM into a BRAM-safe LSU request stage with a one-entry ordinary-RAM store skid slot, strict drain rules before loads/MMIO/ordered instructions, precise RVFI retirement for enqueued stores, and a single registered source for dmem write enables.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace raw dmem-ready freezing with MEM-provided LSU stall signals so stores can retire into the skid slot while loads, full-slot conflicts, MMIO markers, and ordered instructions still hold the pipeline.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire LSU stall, store-slot status, and dmem-ready control between MEM and the hazard unit without changing the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add any compact control or payload fields needed to identify ordered operations and carry store request metadata cleanly through the pipeline.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror any core_pkg.sv bundle additions for alphabetic build flows that compile this guarded shim first.\"\n  - file: test/test_store_buffer.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for RAM store drain under dmem stalls, load-after-store ordering, store-after-store backpressure, MMIO marker bypass, and EBREAK/FENCE drain behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
