{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r1s0 (improvement, +825.33%): Moving rare DIV/REM operations from the one-cycle ALU output mux into an EX-stage iterative unit can dramatically improve FPGA Fmax because CoreMark keeps the common ADD/address path hot while the deep divider cone mostly only taxes timing.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r1s1 (regression, +731.68%): Moving hot MUL/MULH operations into the same EX-holding multi-cycle path as DIV/REM can swamp any ALU timing relief on CoreMark; keep multiplier latency hidden or separately pipelined when only the divider cone is the cold timing problem.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 (regression, +3.63%): A tiny IF-side BHT/direct-JAL next-PC predictor can regress CoreMark when its `io_imemData` decode and target mux sit on the fetch PC-update path, because the added timing/metadata cost can outweigh recovered taken-back-edge flushes.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r2s0 (regression, -10.30%): A dedicated LOAD/STORE address adder can regress CoreMark when it duplicates the same rs1+imm carry chain and adds EX result-select routing instead of removing the true regfile-to-dmem address bottleneck.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Retiming branch resolution into EX2 requires redirect kills to flush the new EX1/EX2 register as well as IF/ID and ID/EX, otherwise wrong-path instructions already in EX1 can survive stall-heavy cosim.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r2s2 (regression, -15.58%): Even without IF instruction decode, a tagged BTB still puts tag/counter/target-table lookup plus a target mux on the fetch PC-update path, and CoreMark's recovered taken-branch bubbles did not repay that timing cost.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r3s0 (regression, +2.55%): Opcode-qualified load-use gating did not repay its added fetched-opcode decode and raw-imem comparator control, implying CoreMark's false immediate-bit load-use bubbles are rarer than the timing cost of making hazard detection more precise.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.56%): A fall-throu\n…[1408 of 4480 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fe2f3138bcf6e2c7]…\n PC-update path more than recovered return bubbles help.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r5s0 (regression, -3.76%): Sharing RV32M multiply variants through one `op`-selected 33x33 signed product can put sign-extension muxing and DSP mapping delay ahead of the hot one-cycle MUL path, outweighing reduced parallel-product area and ALU result mux fan-in.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r5s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Gowin's byte-lane `dmem` inference is sensitive to the whole wrapper-visible address/control cone; a registered store request still exploded `dmem_3` into 16,384 DFFs when `dmem_addr` muxed pending stores with live loads, so store skid buffers need an unchanged RAM port shape, not just an un-muxed `dmem_wen`.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r5s2 (regression, -19.80%): A two-entry loop target cache still regressed because even registered EX updates leave IF doing partial-PC tag compares and a predicted-target mux on the next-PC path, so CoreMark loop-back wins do not justify fetch-path prediction logic here.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
