{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n \\\"category: structural|\\\\\\\"category\\\\\\\":\\\\s*\\\\\\\"structural\\\\\\\"|structural|pipeline|stage|stall|forward|hazard|timing|worst path\\\" cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative divide off ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline timing report shows the critical path running through the forwarding logic into the EX-stage ALU, with an extremely low 5.442 MHz Fmax. The ALU currently contains fully combinational RV32M multiply, divide, and remainder logic in the same output mux used by ordinary ADD/address operations, so the FPGA timing engine must close paths through very deep arithmetic even when CoreMark mostly needs simple integer and memory-address operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline timing report shows the critical path running through the forwarding logic into the EX-stage ALU, with an extremely low 5.442 MHz Fmax. The ALU currently contains fully combinational RV32M multiply, divide, and remainder logic in the same output mux used by ordinary ADD/address operations, so the FPGA timing engine must close paths through very deep arithmetic even when CoreMark mostly needs simple integer and memory-address operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the single-cycle combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU path with a small iterative divider/remainder unit that stalls the in-order pipeline only while one of those rare operations is in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare and MUL/MULH variants on the normal one-cycle ALU path, preserving CoreMark IPC for the common hot instructions while removing the divider cone from the globally timed ALU output. This should substantially improve Fmax on Tang Nano 20K; any extra cycles should be negligible unless the workload is division-heavy.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 25, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove hardware / and % operators from the combinational ALU and leave only fast one-cycle operations plus the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, launch an iterative divide operation, hold EX/MEM until the result is ready\n…[73187 of 76259 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ca3031886c3a8ba5]…\nta_pct\": -19.8, \"vs_baseline\": 642.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 38.633, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4295401, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2987, \"ff\": 778, \"seeds\": [38.617, 38.633, 38.633], \"perturbations\": [[0, 38.617, 26], [1, 38.633, 28], [2, 38.633, 28]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_7_s1\", \"slack_ns\": -20.895, \"arrival_ns\": 31.19, \"logic_cells\": 70, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_hazard\", \"cpu/u_id\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 26, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-04T03:26:47.933404Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r5s2 (regression, -19.80%): A two-entry loop target cache still regressed because even registered EX updates leave IF doing partial-PC tag compares and a predicted-target mux on the next-PC path, so CoreMark loop-back wins do not justify fetch-path prediction logic here.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
