{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"structural\\\") | \\\"--- \\\\(.id) \\\\(.title) [\\\\(.outcome)]\\\\nHYP: \\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES: \\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "--- hyp-20261004-002-r1s1 Move RV32M ops into a multi-cycle execute unit [regression]\nHYP: Split execute into a short single-cycle RV32I ALU/branch path plus a\ndedicated multi-cycle RV32M unit. ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare/address and\nbranch resolution continue to flow through the normal EX/MEM register, while\nMUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU start an M-unit transaction and hold\nthe front of the pipeline until the result is ready. MUL can use registered\nDSP-friendly partial products or a small pipelined multiplier; DIV/REM should\nuse a 32-step iterative divider with signed pre/post processing for exact\nRV32M semantics. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, keep the existing one-cycle\nalgebraic substitutes so the formal depth remains tractable. Removing\ncombinational multiply/divide/remainder from the always-active ALU should\ncollapse the worst path to forwarding plus a simple ALU/branch cone, giving a\nlarge Fmax increase with limited CoreMark IPC loss.\n\nNOTES: \n\n--- hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 Retimed two-stage execute path [broken]\nHYP: Split the existing EX stage into EX1 and EX2. EX1 performs operand selection,\nMEM/WB and EX/MEM forwarding, branch compare input preparation, store-data\ncapture, and iterative-divider launch/hold bookkeeping, then registers the\nselected operands, immediate, decoded control, and PC. EX2 contains the\nactual RV32I ALU/address adder, one-cycle multiplier path, branch/jump target\ncalculation, misalignment checks, and the existing EX/MEM register update.\n\nThis adds one pipeline stage to the execute side and requires forwarding from\nthe new EX2/MEM boundary plus MEM/WB back to EX1, with load-use and\nlong-latency stalls updated accordingly. Taken branches and jumps gain one\ncycle of penalty, but the dominant FPGA path should shrink from\nregfile/forward muxes plus ALU/multiply output selection to one of those\nhalves. If Fmax rises by roughly 15-25%, the extra control-flow bubbles should\nstill clear the 4.6% acceptance threshold on CoreMark.\n\nNOTES: \n\n--- hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 Decoupled sequential fe\n…[1971 of 5043 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f778558d656829cd]…\n tiny LSU request stage with a one-entry store skid slot that is the only source allowed to drive RAM store writes. Ordinary data-RAM stores enqueue their aligned address, replicated data, mask, and RVFI metadata into the slot, retire in order, and allow younger non-memory instructions to continue while the slot drains when io_dmemReady goes high. Loads, following stores, FENCE/EBREAK/SYSTEM, and MMIO/marker stores must wait until the slot is empty, and MMIO stores must complete directly on the external bus so CoreMark timing markers stay precise.\n\nTo avoid repeating the placement failure, do not priority-mux a replayed store with a live store on io_dmemWEn/io_dmemAddr. Instead drive the dmem write port from one registered store-request record, keep io_dmemWEn as either that record's byte mask or zero, and let the current EX/MEM path feed only the enqueue side. This preserves the wrapper's canonical single write-port RAM pattern while hiding store stalls behind independent ALU/control instructions.\n\nNOTES: \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
