{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r6s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Retimed redirect squash path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is no longer limited by the old divider cone, but the\n  recent timing reports repeatedly show paths that leave EX, pass through IF\n  or hazard control, and return to the ID/EX register enable/flush logic. Prior\n  structural attempts that added fetch queues, predictors, or store buffers\n  lost Fmax or disturbed data RAM inference, so the next structural change\n  should remove control feedback instead of adding a new buffered subsystem.\nhypothesis: |\n  Convert taken branch and jump recovery from a same-cycle ID/EX flush into a\n  one-cycle delayed squash of the younger instruction after it has entered EX.\n  On a redirect cycle, keep the existing immediate PC redirect and IF NOP\n  emission, but stop routing redirect through the hazard unit into id_stage's\n  flush decision. Instead, arm a small squash_ex flag only when ID is allowed to\n  advance; on the following cycle ex_stage treats that younger wrong-path\n  payload as invalid and suppresses all side effects and secondary redirects.\n\n  This keeps the visible branch penalty effectively unchanged: the cycle after\n  a redirect is still an EX bubble while the target instruction is decoded.\n  The benefit is structural timing relief, because the hot branch comparator\n  and redirect target path no longer have to close through hazard_unit back\n  into the ID/EX register's synchronous clear/enable controls. Dmem stalls and\n  divider holds must retain their existing precedence by arming the squash only\n  when the younger instruction actually advances, so no hard invariant is\n  weakened.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Add a registered squash_ex control bit that is armed by redirects only when ID advances, wire it into execute, and keep the nret=1 IO/RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Remove redirect from the same-cycle ID/EX flush path while preserving load-use bubbles, dmem/divider holds, imem stalls, and IF redirect flushing semantics.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Accept a squash input that converts the current ID/EX payload into an invalid bubble, suppressing reg writes, memory requests, traps, and further redirects for wrong-path instructions.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Keep redirect priority for PC update and NOP emission, but avoid relying on hazard_unit redirect feedback for fetch-side flushing.\"\n  - file: test/test_redirect_squash.py\n    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for taken branches and jumps coincident with imem stalls, dmem stalls, and divider holds to ensure the delayed squash kills exactly the wrong-path instruction.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
