{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r7s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r7s1\ntitle: \"Corrected two-stage execute retime\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is still limited by the register/forwarding-to-ALU/data-memory timing cone after DIV/REM were removed from the one-cycle ALU. Most later structural work added frontend prediction, queues, or LSU replay muxes and lost Fmax or disturbed block-RAM inference. The one structural idea that directly attacks the hot execute cone, splitting EX into two timed stages, was not evaluated for fitness because wrong-path instructions already sitting in the new execute register were not killed on redirects.\nhypothesis: |\n  Reintroduce a two-stage execute pipeline, but make precise valid/kill behavior the first-class design constraint. EX1 should do forwarding selection and latch the chosen operands, immediate, PC, instruction, decoded control, and store data. EX2 should contain the ALU, one-cycle multiplier, branch/jump target calculation, misalignment checks, iterative divider capture, and the existing EX/MEM register update.\n\n  On every taken branch, jump, misprediction-equivalent redirect, illegal target trap, or reset/flush event, the control logic must kill both younger frontend instructions and any wrong-path payload already resident in the EX1/EX2 register. Dmem stalls and divider stalls must hold the correct stage contents rather than replaying or dropping requests. Forwarding should target EX1 from the registered EX2/MEM result and MEM/WB result; load-use logic should account for the extra stage so dependent loads still wait for real load data.\n\n  This pays one additional branch/jump recovery cycle, but CoreMark's present score appears Fmax-limited and prior predictor/queue attempts suggest the fetch side is the wrong place to spend logic. If the execute split raises Fmax by about 15% while keeping steady-state throughput at one instruction per cycle for independent ALU and memory operations, it can clear the 4.6% replacement threshold even with modest extra control-flow bubbles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add an EX1/EX2 bundle carrying selected operands, store data, immediate, PC, control, instruction, register addresses, destination, and valid state.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the new EX1/EX2 bundle in the alphabetical ordering shim so all build flows see the same typedefs.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Split execute internally into an EX1 operand/forwarding register and an EX2 ALU/branch/divider/output stage, with explicit kill support for wrong-path EX1 payloads.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Retarget bypass selection to the EX1 source stage, forwarding from registered EX2/MEM and MEM/WB producers without exposing unavailable load data.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Update load-use, redirect, dmem stall, imem stall, and divider hold rules for the extra execute register, including flushing the EX1/EX2 register on redirects.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the retimed execute-stage stall, flush, and forwarding interfaces while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO and RVFI retirement contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_execute_retime.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb tests for taken branches and jumps with wrong-path instructions in EX1, plus load-use and dmem/divider stalls through the new stage.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
