{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r7s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-001-r8s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r8s1\\\") | .id + \\\"\\\\nTITLE: \\\" + .title + \\\"\\\\nHYP: \\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nCRIT: \\\" + (.critical_path|tostring) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r7s1\nTITLE: Corrected two-stage execute retime\nHYP: Reintroduce a two-stage execute pipeline, but make precise valid/kill behavior the first-class design constraint. EX1 should do forwarding selection and latch the chosen operands, immediate, PC, instruction, decoded control, and store data. EX2 should contain the ALU, one-cycle multiplier, branch/jump target calculation, misalignment checks, iterative divider capture, and the existing EX/MEM register update.\n\nOn every taken branch, jump, misprediction-equivalent redirect, illegal target trap, or reset/flush event, the control logic must kill both younger frontend instructions and any wrong-path payload already resident in the EX1/EX2 register. Dmem stalls and divider stalls must hold the correct stage contents rather than replaying or dropping requests. Forwarding should target EX1 from the registered EX2/MEM result and MEM/WB result; load-use logic should account for the extra stage so dependent loads still wait for real load data.\n\nThis pays one additional branch/jump recovery cycle, but CoreMark's present score appears Fmax-limited and prior predictor/queue attempts suggest the fetch side is the wrong place to spend logic. If the execute split raises Fmax by about 15% while keeping steady-state throughput at one instruction per cycle for independent ALU and memory operations, it can clear the 4.6% replacement threshold even with modest extra control-flow bubbles.\n\nCRIT: {\"from\":\"cpu/u_ex/ex1_q.ctrl.is_auipc_s0\",\"to\":\"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-10.666,\"arrival_ns\":20.961,\"logic_cells\":10,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n\nhyp-20261004-001-r8s0\nTITLE: Preselect EX2 ALU operands\nHYP: Add registered ALU operand fields to the EX1/EX2 payload. EX1 already has\nthe forwarded rs1/rs2 values, PC, immediate, and decoded control, so it can\nprecompute and latch `alu_a = is_auipc ? pc : rs1` and\n`alu_b = alu_src ? imm : rs2`. EX2 then feeds the ALU directly from these\nregistered operand fields instead of selecting operands in the same cycle\nas the ADD/address carry chain.\n\nThis should shorten the current `is_auipc`-to-dmem critical path without\nadding frontend prediction, LSU buffering, store replay muxes, or extra\nCoreMark cycles. The existing rs1/rs2 payload fields should remain intact\nfor branch compares, JALR target calculation, divider operands, store data,\nforwarding visibility, and RVFI reporting; only the ALU input path is\nretimed.\n\nCRIT: {\"from\":\"cpu/u_ex/ex1_q.rs2_val_0_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_ER_s196\",\"slack_ns\":-8.179,\"arrival_ns\":18.474,\"logic_cells\":41,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_rf\"]}\n\nhyp-20261004-002-r8s1\nTITLE: Preselect ALU operands in EX1\nHYP: Extend the EX1/EX2 bundle with already-selected ALU operands. EX1 should apply\nthe existing forwarding muxes and then latch alu_a = AUIPC ? pc : rs1 and\nalu_b = alu_src ? imm : rs2 alongside the original rs1/rs2 values needed for\nbranch compares, stores, divider operands, and RVFI. EX2 then feeds the ALU\ndirectly from those registered operands instead of recomputing the AUIPC and\nimmediate/source muxes on the same side of the boundary as the adder, multiplier\nresult mux, branch logic, and EX/MEM capture.\n\nThis is a structural retime rather than a new predictor or memory subsystem:\nit should not change CoreMark cycle count, RVFI retirement behavior, divider\nsemantics, or the top-level memory contract. By removing the control-bit to\nadder operand mux from the current worst EX2 path, the design may gain enough\nFmax to clear the replacement threshold while preserving the steady-state IPC\nof the corrected two-stage execute core.\n\nCRIT: {\"from\":\"cpu/u_ex/ex1_q.rs2_val_0_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_ER_s196\",\"slack_ns\":-8.18,\"arrival_ns\":18.474,\"logic_cells\":41,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_rf\"]}\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
