{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r4s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-003-r4s2\ntitle: \"Tiny return-stack predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The prior predictor attempts all tried to steer the next fetch for direct\n  branches or BTB hits and paid for that choice on the Tang Nano 20K fetch\n  timing path. CoreMark still contains call/return control flow, and every\n  JALR return currently behaves like any other jump: fetch goes to pc+4 until\n  EX resolves the real target and flushes the wrong-path instruction.\n\n  A return-address stack is a more specialized predictor with a much smaller\n  IF cost than a direct branch predictor. The predicted target is already a\n  registered stack top, so IF only needs a canonical-return recognizer and a\n  mux, not an immediate target adder, BHT/BTB table lookup, or tag compare.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a tiny 4-entry return-address stack that predicts only canonical RV32I\n  returns, such as `jalr x0, 0(x1)` and `jalr x0, 0(x5)`. Direct calls and\n  link-register JALR calls push their resolved return address when they reach\n  EX; real returns pop in EX. IF predicts a return taken only when the fetched\n  instruction exactly matches a canonical return encoding and the stack is\n  non-empty, then selects the registered stack top as the next PC.\n\n  Carry a predicted-return bit and predicted target with the instruction so\n  EX can suppress the normal jump redirect when the RAS prediction was correct\n  and can recover to the resolved JALR target when it was wrong. This should\n  recover recurring CoreMark return bubbles with substantially less Fmax risk\n  than previous predictors because there is no branch-immediate decode, target\n  adder, saturating counter, tag RAM, or learned target lookup on the fetch\n  next-PC path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/return_stack.sv\n    description: \"Add a tiny registered return-address stack with push/pop ports from EX and a stack-top prediction output for IF.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add predicted-return and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can validate the RAS prediction.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the bundle metadata additions for alphabetic build flows that compile this guarded shim first.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Recognize canonical JALR return encodings, use the RAS stack top as the predicted next PC when valid, and emit prediction metadata while keeping redirect priority highest.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Push call return addresses, pop real returns, suppress redirects for correct return predictions, and redirect on RAS mispredictions.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate and wire the return stack between IF and EX without changing the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
