{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis | tostring) + \\\"\\\\n---\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261004-003-r1s2\nAdd a small direct-branch next-PC predictor in IF: a 64-entry 2-bit saturating BHT indexed by pc[7:2], with direct JALs predicted taken immediately and conditional branches predicted taken only when the counter says taken and the instruction immediate supplies a valid aligned target. Carry predicted_taken and predicted_target through ID/EX, update the counter in EX from the actual branch result, and redirect only when the predicted next PC differs from the resolved next PC. This should remove most recurring loop-back flushes in CoreMark while adding only a modest LUT/FF footprint and no changes to RVFI retirement semantics.\n\n---\nhyp-20261004-003-r2s2\nAdd a tiny direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer, for example 16 or 32\nentries, with valid, partial PC tag, target, and a 2-bit direction counter.\nIF should consult it using only the current PC: on a tag hit with a taken\ncounter, next_pc becomes the stored target; otherwise next_pc remains pc+4.\nEX resolves the real next PC as today, updates the entry for taken direct\nbranches/JAL/JALR and the saturating counter for conditional branches, and\nredirects/flushed only when the predicted next PC differs from the actual\nnext PC. This deliberately avoids any io_imemData opcode/immediate decode in\nIF, so the added timing is a small PC-indexed table read and mux rather than\nan instruction-dependent target-generation path. Warm-up misses still behave\nlike the current core, while recurring CoreMark loop back edges and direct\njumps should retire with fewer bubbles.\n\n---\nhyp-20261004-003-r3s2\nAdd a zero-state static direct-control predictor in IF: predict conditional\nbranches with negative B-immediates taken, predict positive/forward\nconditional branches not taken, and predict direct JAL taken. The predictor\nforms the direct target from the fetched instruction and PC, carries only a\npredicted-taken bit through ID/EX, and lets EX recover when the actual branch\ndirection differs. Because the IF-computed target uses the same direct\nimmediate formula as EX, EX does not need a 32-bit predicted-target compare;\nit can suppress redirects for correctly predicted taken direct branches and\nJALs using opcode/control plus the predicted-taken flag. This should recover\nthe recurring CoreMark loop-back bubble with much less LUT, FF, and timing\ncost than a BHT/BTB.\n\n---\nhyp-20261004-003-r4s2\nAdd a tiny 4-entry return-address stack that predicts only canonical RV32I\nreturns, such as `jalr x0, 0(x1)` and `jalr x0, 0(x5)`. Direct calls and\nlink-register JALR calls push their resolved return address when they reach\nEX; real returns pop in EX. IF predicts a return taken only when the fetched\ninstruction exactly matches a canonical return encoding and the stack is\nnon-empty, then selects the registered stack top as the next PC.\n\nCarry a predicted-return bit and predicted target with the instruction so\nEX can suppress the normal jump redirect when the RAS prediction was correct\nand can recover to the resolved JALR target when it was wrong. This should\nrecover recurring CoreMark return bubbles with substantially less Fmax risk\nthan previous predictors because there is no branch-immediate decode, target\nadder, saturating counter, tag RAM, or learned target lookup on the fetch\nnext-PC path.\n\n---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
