{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r5s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-003-r5s2\ntitle: \"Two-entry loop target cache\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior predictor attempts proved that CoreMark has roughly 5-6% cycle-count\n  upside from avoiding recurring taken-control redirects, but they lost more\n  Fmax than they gained because instruction decode, target adders, table\n  lookup, or RAS update logic landed on the fetch next-PC path. The most\n  promising remaining predictor angle is therefore a much smaller learned\n  target structure that specializes in loop back edges and avoids any\n  combinational update path from EX back into IF.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a two-entry loop target cache that allocates only when EX resolves a\n  taken direct backward branch or direct JAL with a low code-space target.\n  Each entry stores a small partial PC tag and a full resolved target. IF\n  compares the current PC against the two registered tags and, on a hit,\n  predicts the registered target; otherwise it fetches pc+4 exactly as today.\n  Updates happen only in clocked state, with no same-cycle write-through from\n  EX into the IF prediction result. The instruction carries predicted-taken\n  and predicted-target metadata to EX, which redirects only when the predicted\n  next PC differs from the resolved next PC; partial-tag aliases and loop exits\n  are therefore correct but pay a normal recovery flush.\n\n  This should recover the hottest CoreMark loop-back bubbles with far less\n  fetch timing cost than the previous BHT, BTB, static immediate predictor, or\n  RAS designs: no IF instruction decode, no IF target adder, no saturating\n  counters, no RAM read mux, and no EX-update bypass through the predictor.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/loop_target_cache.sv\n    description: \"Add a two-entry registered loop target cache with partial PC tags, full resolved targets, clocked EX updates, and no combinational update bypass into IF prediction.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted-taken and predicted-target metadata used to validate or recover the loop-target prediction.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the bundle metadata additions for alphabetical build flows that compile this guarded shim before core_pkg.sv.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the cache hit and registered target as the predicted next PC while preserving redirect priority, imem stall behavior, and NOP emission on recovery.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch the prediction metadata alongside the decoded instruction without changing decoder legality or RVFI source fields.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare predicted and resolved next PC, suppress redirects on correct loop predictions, recover on aliases or loop exits, and emit cache update information for taken direct backward branches and direct JALs.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate and wire the loop target cache between IF and EX while keeping the nret=1 top-level IO and RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
