{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\"|Tiny BHT|Tagged BTB|backward|return-stack|loop target' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny BHT next-PC predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline always fetches pc+4 and resolves every taken branch or jump in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-instruction flush on each taken back edge. CoreMark's hot code has many direct loop branches and unconditional JAL control transfers whose targets are available from the fetched instruction bits, making them good candidates for a very small predictor that improves iterations per cycle without touching the memory contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline always fetches pc+4 and resolves every taken branch or jump in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-instruction flush on each taken back edge. CoreMark's hot code has many direct loop branches and unconditional JAL control transfers whose targets are available from the fetched instruction bits, making them good candidates for a very small predictor that improves iterations per cycle without touching the memory contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-branch next-PC predictor in IF: a 64-entry 2-bit saturating BHT indexed by pc[7:2], with direct JALs predicted taken immediately and conditional branches predicted taken only when the counter says taken and the instruction immediate supplies a valid aligned target. Carry predicted_taken and predicted_target through ID/EX, update the counter in EX from the actual branch result, and redirect only when the predicted next PC differs from the resolved next PC. This should remove most recurring loop-back flushes in CoreMark while adding only a modest LUT/FF footprint and no changes to RVFI retirement semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny 64-entry 2-bit BHT next-PC predictor for direct conditional branches, plus combinational JAL target prediction and EX-stage update inputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID, ID/EX, and\n…[25355 of 28427 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d1cdf37c7b89acda]…\nta_pct\": -19.8, \"vs_baseline\": 642.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 38.633, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4295401, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2987, \"ff\": 778, \"seeds\": [38.617, 38.633, 38.633], \"perturbations\": [[0, 38.617, 26], [1, 38.633, 28], [2, 38.633, 28]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_7_s1\", \"slack_ns\": -20.895, \"arrival_ns\": 31.19, \"logic_cells\": 70, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_hazard\", \"cpu/u_id\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 26, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-04T03:26:47.933404Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r5s2 (regression, -19.80%): A two-entry loop target cache still regressed because even registered EX updates leave IF doing partial-PC tag compares and a predicted-target mux on the next-PC path, so CoreMark loop-back wins do not justify fetch-path prediction logic here.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
