{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r7s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-003-r7s2\ntitle: \"Tiny loop replay predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior predictor attempts recovered roughly five to six percent of CoreMark\n  cycles, but each lost more Fmax than it gained by putting instruction decode,\n  tag lookup, target comparison, or a target mux on the IF next-PC path. The\n  current champion is already Fmax-sensitive, so another general branch\n  predictor is unlikely to clear the acceptance threshold.\n\n  CoreMark also runs with about twenty-two percent random instruction-memory\n  stalls. A predictor that recognizes a hot short loop and locally replays its\n  instruction body can attack both taken-branch bubbles and repeated imem\n  stalls, while avoiding arbitrary IF target generation.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a tiny loop replay predictor for short direct backward loops. During\n  normal fetch, keep a rolling history of the most recent valid aligned\n  instructions and PCs. When EX resolves a taken direct backward conditional\n  branch whose target and contiguous body are still in that history, allocate a\n  replay entry containing the loop body, target PC, branch PC, and loop length.\n\n  On later visits to the loop target, IF enters replay mode and feeds ID from\n  the registered loop body using a small counter instead of imemData. The loop\n  branch at the end is predicted taken by resetting the replay counter to the\n  first body instruction; EX validates the real outcome and recovers to pc+4\n  on loop exit or any mismatch. During replay, imemReady is ignored for the\n  buffered instructions, and io_imemAddr can stay on a simple registered\n  fall-through or prefetch address rather than selecting a learned target.\n\n  This is a predictor, but it is deliberately not another BHT/BTB/RAS in the\n  failed style: the hot prediction is \"continue/restart this validated local\n  loop stream\", not \"choose an arbitrary target in IF\". Correct hot loops\n  should avoid recurring redirect bubbles and most instruction-bus stalls\n  inside the body; aliases, exits, interrupts of replay by dmem/divider stalls,\n  and unsupported loop shapes fall back to the existing precise EX redirect\n  behavior without weakening RVFI, trap, or memory invariants.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/loop_replay_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Add a small registered loop-body history and replay predictor that allocates short direct backward loops from EX-resolved branch outcomes and serves buffered instructions by replay index.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX metadata with replay-active, replay-loop-end, and predicted-restart state needed for EX validation and RVFI pc tracking.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the new replay metadata fields for alphabetical build flows that compile the guarded shim before core_pkg.sv.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Select registered replay instructions and PCs when the loop predictor is active, ignore imemReady for replayed instructions, and keep real EX redirect recovery highest priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Allocate replay entries from taken direct backward branches, validate replay-end predictions, suppress redirects for correct predicted loop iterations, and recover precisely on loop exits or mismatches.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Treat replayed instructions as fetch-ready while preserving load-use, dmem, divider, and recovery stall precedence.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate and wire the loop replay predictor between IF and EX without changing the nret=1 top-level IO or memory interface.\"\n  - file: test/test_loop_replay.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for a short taken loop, loop exit recovery, imem stalls during replay, and replay cancellation across dmem or divider holds.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
