on, -19.80%): A two-entry loop target cache still regressed because even registered EX updates leave IF doing partial-PC tag compares and a predicted-target mux on the next-PC path, so CoreMark loop-back wins do not justify fetch-path prediction logic here.\nhyp-20261004-003-r6s2\tDecode-stage static control predictor\tregression\t-29.45\t33.702\t4259699\t88\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r6s2 (regression, -29.45%): Decode-stage static direct-control prediction still regressed because the ID `predict_redirect`/`pred_target` feedback into IF plus EX `resolved_next_pc` versus `predicted_next_pc` validation moved timing pressure into decode and recovery control, so avoiding IF tables is not enough if prediction still widens the PC-update path.\nhyp-20261004-003-r7s2\tTiny loop replay predictor\tregression\t-6.13\t43.206\t4104094\t34\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r7s2 (regression, -6.13%): Tiny loop replay still regressed because `replay_entry_instrs`/PC body muxing, replay metadata through IF/ID/ID/EX, and `imem_ready` bypass control added more frontend/recovery timing cost than CoreMark's repeated imem-stall and back-edge bubbles repaid.\nhyp-20261004-003-r8s2\tIDEX early backedge predictor\tregression\t-15.61\t53.399\t5267491\t74\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r8s2 (regression, -15.61%): ID/EX-only backedge prediction still regressed because `early_redirect` feedback into the global PC/hazard path plus EX2 `resolved_next_pc` versus `predicted_next_pc` validation costs more than CoreMark recovers from one-stage-earlier loop redirects.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
