{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r9s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-003-r9s2\ntitle: \"Registered branch repair predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst path runs from EX branch data back through the\n  frontend and decode/regfile logic. Earlier BHT, BTB, RAS, loop replay, and\n  early backedge predictors all lost because they added target selection or\n  validation logic to the fetch path. A predictor hypothesis that removes\n  conditional-branch compare feedback from IF, rather than adding a new target\n  lookup, is the least risky remaining predictor angle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the frontend's prediction policy as static not-taken for conditional\n  branches, but register the taken-branch repair redirect for one cycle before\n  it reaches IF and ID. Direct JAL and JALR should continue to redirect\n  immediately, because their targets do not depend on an rs1-vs-rs2 branch\n  compare and they are architecturally always taken. For conditional branches,\n  EX2 still computes the branch condition and target in the normal place, but\n  the result only drives a small local kill for the younger instruction entering\n  EX1; the PC update, IF flush, and ID flush consume a registered\n  branch_redirect_q on the next cycle.\n\n  This is effectively a static not-taken predictor with a retimed repair path:\n  taken conditional branches pay one extra recovery cycle, while not-taken\n  branches remain free and the hot EX compare no longer feeds the long\n  EX-to-IF-to-ID/regfile combinational path reported by timing. If the removed\n  feedback lifts Fmax by more than the extra taken-loop penalty costs in\n  CoreMark IPC, the net CoreMark iterations/second should clear the acceptance\n  threshold without adding any fetch-side tables, instruction decode, or target\n  muxes.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Split resolved control outputs into immediate jump redirect and conditional branch redirect, keeping a local same-cycle younger-EX1 kill for taken conditional branches.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Register the conditional branch repair redirect for one cycle, combine it with immediate jump redirects for IF PC updates, and keep the nret=1 RVFI interface unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Separate local EX1 kill control from frontend redirect flush control so conditional branch compares no longer drive IF and ID in the same cycle.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Consume only the combined registered-branch-or-immediate-jump redirect path, preserving redirect priority over stalls and otherwise sequential fetch.\"\n  - file: test/test_registered_branch_repair.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for taken conditional branch repair, not-taken fall-through, immediate JAL/JALR redirects, and dmem/imem stall interactions.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
